  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "epVo7Il0aJSXN5zbYibC2cV6o64=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:35.595Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Processors run in parallel, each wrapped in its own try/catch. When `onOperations` throws, the manager does three things and then stops:\n\n1. The processor moves to `status: \"errored\"`.\n2. `lastError` and `lastErrorTimestamp` are recorded on its `TrackedProcessor`.\n3. **Its cursor stops advancing.**\n\nOther processors are unaffected \u2014 a failure is contained to one processor on one drive, not the batch and not the reactor. The frozen cursor is the deliberate half of the design: because `lastOrdinal` does not move past the failing batch, no operations are lost. Once the processor is revived it resumes from exactly where it broke.\n\nThe part that surprises people is that **there is no automatic retry.** An errored processor stays errored and is skipped on every later batch, indefinitely. Recovery is explicitly triggered, by one of:\n\n- `tracked.retry()` \u2014 re-activates the processor and re-runs backfill from its frozen cursor.\n- Re-registering its factory \u2014 registering the same `identifier` unregisters the prior factory first, so this is idempotent.\n\nNothing about this is visible unless you look. Inspect via the manager: `get(processorId)` for one, `getAll()` for every tracked processor across all drives.\n\n```typescript\ntype TrackedProcessor = {\n  processorId: string;       // `${factoryId}:${driveId}:${index}`\n  factoryId: string;\n  driveId: string;\n  processorIndex: number;\n  record: ProcessorRecord;\n  lastOrdinal: number;\n  status: ProcessorStatus;   // \"active\" | \"errored\"\n  lastError: string | undefined;\n  lastErrorTimestamp: Date | undefined;\n  retry: () => Promise<void>;\n};\n```\n\nPractically, this means any production processor needs a health check that walks `getAll()` looking for `status: \"errored\"` \u2014 otherwise a read model quietly stops updating and the only symptom is stale data. Note that `onDisconnect` is treated differently: a throw there is caught and logged, never propagated, and does not error the processor.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:34:34.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:35.595Z"
    }
  },
